//
//! The build half of Lua's runtime-loading route: three modules and one
//! generated file.
//!
//! An SDK that ships the runtime route calls `createModule` in place of
//! `translate.createModule()` and imports what comes back as its `c` module.
//! Nothing here links the native library. That is the point: in this
//! configuration the symbols arrive through the platform loader at run time,
//! and linking the archive as well would make the linker demand them before
//! `load` ever runs.
//!
//!   rt        runtime/loader.zig, the reusable core. Pure Zig; imported by the
//!             facade in both configurations, because `LoadSource` and
//!             `LoadError` are this SDK's public types and must be the same
//!             types whichever configuration is built.
//!   library   runtime/library.zig, this SDK's descriptor: the namespaced
//!             synchronization binding and the one load-time check.
//!   dispatch  generated by runtime/generate.zig from the real translation
//!             output: the table, the wrappers, `load`, and the re-exported
//!             translated namespace. It imports `rt`, `library` and the
//!             generator's patched translation as `c_native`, which imports the
//!             dispatch back as `c_routed`.
//!   sync.c    compiled into the dispatch with `-DRT_SYNC_PREFIX=lua_rt`, so
//!             its symbols cannot collide with another SDK's.
//!
//! ## The one thing a caller must not do
//!
//! Do not link the compiled C archive into a module that imports this dispatch.
//! The facade's `c` import is the dispatch, and nothing else in the graph should
//! reference the library, or the linker resolves the symbols statically and the
//! runtime route silently becomes a linked one.
//!
//! ## What is routed, and what is not
//!
//! The translated header for this SDK is `zig/translate.h`, which includes
//! exactly `<lua.h>`, `<lauxlib.h>` and `<lualib.h>` and defines no macro. Those
//! three headers declare 156 functions and one exported data object,
//! `lua_ident` (`src/lua.h:157`). Every one of those 157 names is routed: a
//! provider that does not export the whole header-declared API is refused
//! rather than half used. `provider_optional` and `provider_excluded` below are
//! therefore empty, and are written out rather than omitted so that adding a
//! header with an optional or deliberately-direct entry point is a change in
//! this file with a name in it.
//!
//! The one header-declared thing that is *not* a Lua symbol is
//! `<lauxlib.h:13`'s `#include <stdio.h>`, which exists for `FILE *` in
//! `luaL_Stream` (`src/lauxlib.h:239-242`). `zig translate-c` emits a
//! declaration for every function it parses in that include closure, whether or
//! not this build calls it, so the translation carries the target libc's whole
//! `stdio` surface alongside Lua's own. Those are the C library's symbols, not
//! the provider's: a `liblua.so` does not export `printf`, and asking a
//! provider for one would either fail the load outright or, worse, resolve it
//! out of the C library the provider itself depends on. They are therefore
//! classified as noise, which keeps the declarations direct.
//!
//! The list below is the union, over the libc families this package can be
//! configured for, of every function and object name that reaches the
//! translation through the include closure of `<stdio.h>`, `<stdarg.h>`,
//! `<stddef.h>`, `<limits.h>` and `<stdint.h>`. Those five are what the three
//! translated headers reach and nothing more: `src/lauxlib.h:12-13` opens
//! `<stddef.h>` and `<stdio.h>`, `src/lua.h:12-13` opens `<stdarg.h>` and
//! `<stddef.h>`, `src/luaconf.h:11-12` opens `<limits.h>`, and
//! `src/luaconf.h:630` opens `<stdint.h>` on the C99 path, which this
//! configuration always takes because `build.zig` never defines
//! `LUA_USE_C89`.
//!
//! It is a union across families on purpose: a name that a given target's libc
//! does not declare simply never appears in that target's translation and the
//! entry costs nothing, while a name that is missing would be routed and would
//! turn every load into a `MissingSymbol`.
//!
//! The families are the ones `lib/libc/include` holds in the pinned Zig
//! distribution for a target this package can be built for:
//! `generic-glibc`, `generic-musl`, `wasm-wasi-musl`, `any-windows-any`,
//! `any-darwin-any`, `generic-freebsd`, `generic-netbsd` and `generic-openbsd`.
//! The names were read out of the translations themselves rather than out of
//! the headers by hand: `zig translate-c` was run over `zig/translate.h` for
//! this package's six `check_targets` (`build.zig:450-457`) and for every
//! target of the published 53-platform matrix, and every `extern` name those
//! translations carry that is not a `lua_`, `luaL_` or `luaopen_` name is
//! below. A name that is a preprocessor macro rather than a declaration cannot
//! appear there at all, because a macro cannot survive into a translation as a
//! declaration.
//!
//! Fourteen cells of that matrix produce no translation from the pinned
//! compiler at all: the nine NetBSD ones, where `translate-c` fails inside
//! `generic-netbsd/stdio.h:151` on the `_Pragma("GCC visibility pop")`
//! `generic-netbsd/sys/cdefs.h:413` expands to, and five of the OpenBSD ones -
//! `arm`, `powerpc`, `mips64`, `mips64el` and `x86` - where
//! `generic-openbsd/sys/types.h:173` redefines `size_t` against the
//! architecture's own `__size_t`. They contribute nothing here because a
//! target that has no translation has no closure to classify, and the build
//! that would need one cannot produce it.
//!
//! The `LUA_USE_*` macros `build.zig` puts on the translation line, and the
//! `LUA_BUILD_AS_DLL` a shared build adds, change nothing: re-running
//! `zig translate-c` with each of them for the target that gets it answers the
//! same set of names, because what they select are branches in upstream's
//! `.c` units - `src/loadlib.c`, `src/lualib.c`, `src/loslib.c` - and the
//! translation reads headers only.

const std = @import("std");

/// The prefix `runtime/sync.c` is compiled with.
///
/// `runtime/library.zig` declares the same prefix in the symbol names it binds.
/// The two must agree, and a build where they do not fails to link, which is
/// the whole error report: there is no runtime path that could hide it.
///
/// A build that needs a second, independent instance of this SDK in one binary
/// should change this value *and* the prefix in `runtime/library.zig`, and then
/// give the second instance its own copy of both files. Nothing else in the
/// package assumes one instance.
const sync_prefix = "lua_rt";

/// `extern` declarations that are the C library's, not Lua's.
///
/// See "What is routed, and what is not" above for where this list comes from
/// and why leaving a name out of it is a load-time failure rather than a
/// detail.
///
/// ### The list is comma-separated, and every line ends with one
///
/// `runtime/generate.zig` reads these three lists through
/// `std.mem.splitScalar(u8, list, ',')` followed by a whitespace trim, and a
/// Zig multiline string literal concatenates its lines with nothing between
/// them. A line that does not end in a comma therefore glues its last name to
/// the first name of the next line into one token, and that token matches
/// neither declaration: both real names are lost and both get routed. This
/// file had exactly that fault - all ninety-one content lines below ended
/// without a delimiter - and it is why a load failed with `MissingSymbol` on
/// names such as `dprintf`, `ferror`, `fgetpos` and `__getdelim` that are
/// plainly the C library's. The commas are load-bearing, not decoration.
///
/// ### The fortified and alias declarations an optimized translation adds
///
/// `translate-c` reads the target's headers under the *build's* configuration,
/// and an optimized build turns `_FORTIFY_SOURCE` on. glibc's
/// `_FORTIFY_SOURCE` block declares a second family of externs an unfortified
/// pass never sees: a `__*_chk` implementation, a `__*_alias` redirect back onto
/// the plain function, and a `__*_chk_warn` sibling. They are declared by the C
/// library, the C library exports them, and `liblua` exports none of them.
///
/// Six were missing from this list and are added, each from the pinned Zig
/// distribution's `lib/libc/include/generic-glibc/bits/stdio2-decl.h`:
/// `__fgets_alias` (`:96`), `__fgets_chk_warn` (`:100`), `__fread_alias`
/// (`:110`), `__fread_chk_warn` (`:114`), `__fread_unlocked_alias` (`:145`)
/// and `__fread_unlocked_chk_warn` (`:149`). Their `__fgets_chk` (`:106`),
/// `__fread_chk` (`:122`) and `__fread_unlocked_chk` (`:157`) siblings were
/// already listed.
///
/// None of the six is a `lua_`, `luaL_` or `luaopen_` name or is declared by
/// `lua.h`, `lauxlib.h` or `lualib.h`, so nothing native-owned is de-routed by
/// listing them, and they are listed by exact name rather than by a `__` prefix
/// rule.
const libc_noise =
